201318246 \u2014 so no volume surge drives the result.\n\n**Partner sets flip completely.** For every group member sampled (323, 1119, 1776, 889, 1553, 1432, 477), the pre-15000 neighbourhood is drawn from a stable pool {55, 68, 118, 139, 441, 722, 788, 794, 901, 1009, 1057, 1145, 1444, 1702, 1840} (steps 05\u201307, 13, 15, 17, 19, 23, 32), while the post-15246 neighbourhood is drawn from a disjoint pool {39, 306, 337, 425, 585, 955, 977, 1008, 1147, 1181, 1247, 1387, 1725, 1734, 1847} (steps 08\u201310, 14, 16, 18, 20, 24). Jaccard overlap \u2248 0 across the boundary, yet each side is internally stable across two later 5000-unit windows (steps 09/10, 20).\n\n**The transition is short, not gradual.** The group is silent in [14246, 16246] (step_31), with 22 events in the preceding bin and 42 in the following bin; node-level probes confirm the last old-pool contacts end by ~13746 (steps 11, 12, 21, 22).\n\n**Confounder controls.** Old-pool volume collapses 59 \u2192 5 \u2192 1 \u2192 1 while new-pool volume jumps 4 \u2192 65 \u2192 84 \u2192 76 (steps 29, 30) \u2014 a substitution, not decay plus independent growth. The union of old+new partners carries 63 vs 70 events on either side (step_28), so total throughput is conserved; and the new partners are not globally hot hubs (degrees 12\u201318 over 30000 units, comparable to the old partners' 13\u201314, step_27), ruling out \"recent popularity\" as the explanation.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_c1b49572\",\n  \"decision\": \"present\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [323, 477, 865, 889, 1039, 1119, 1389, 1432, 1530, 1553, 1639, 1776],\n  \"window\": [246, 30246],\n  \"confidence\": 0.91,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n 